﻿Implemented already, so this is closed as completed.

`injectParentIssue` in `src/entrypoints/tools/mcp/github.ts` writes `Closes #<parent>` into every parented pull request body, unconditionally:

```ts
const closes = `${closesLine(Number(parent))}\n`;
const originAgent = (process.env.AGENT ?? "").trim();
const originLine = originAgent ? `${ORIGIN_AGENT_TAG.write(originAgent)}\n` : "";
return `${reviewerLine}${PARENT_ISSUE_TAG.write(Number(parent))}\n${originLine}${closes}${body}`;
```

The two halves this issue asked about are both in place:

- **The tag read is what decides, not the closing keyword.** `parse_pr_metadata.ts` reads `PARENT_ISSUE_TAG` for the parent and `closedIssueNumber` for the sub-issue — two different questions, neither derived from the other. So injecting `Closes #N` cannot break sub-issue detection.
- **The injection is not conditional on the body.** It used to check for an existing closing keyword first; that check is gone, and `refuseClosingKeywords` above guarantees an agent wrote none of its own. A second `Closes #N` can no longer arrive, which is what the old check existed for.

The ingredient this issue expected to add — "keep `claimsToClose` reading the parent-issue tag too" — is not needed: `closedIssueNumber` is used only for the sub-issue number, and `PARENT_ISSUE_TAG` for the parent, so there is no path where the closing keyword is asked about the parent.

The consequence noted here does still hold — a stacked pull request's body has no native "linked pull request" line in GitHub's UI, because GitHub does not make the link for a PR whose base is not the default branch. Agents read the tag, which is why it is there; a person reading the issue page does not get the link. That is a separate, still-open question (see #96 for the merge-gate side of it) rather than part of this issue.

